  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Profit Bases, Ratios and Algebraic Price Relations",
    "description": "Translate unusual statements such as profit as a fraction of S.P., C.P.:S.P. ratios, doubled selling price and symbolic price relations.",
    "teachingApproach": "Teach the learner to label the base hidden in unusual wording. Rebase profit/loss stated on S.P., use C.P.:S.P. ratios, form equations for doubled prices and symbolic relations, and end with mixed-base capstones.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12, Profit and Loss. Approved scope: Cover advanced wording where the usual percentage base is disguised. Teach profit or loss stated as a fraction/percentage of S.P., convert between profit-on-S.P. and profit-on-C.P., use C.P.:S.P. ratios, and solve relations such as doubling S.P. triples profit or a numerical profit percent equals the C.P. Include symbolic forms with p, q and r, equal percentage profit/loss at two prices, and short equations created from changed selling prices. The core habit is to label C.P., S.P. and profit amount before forming an equation."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn profit bases, ratios and algebraic price relations.\n\n‘Profit is 25% of selling price’ and ‘profit is 25%’ are not the same statement. The percentage base decides the whole calculation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Profit percentage is normally on C.P., but some questions deliberately say profit is a fraction of S.P. Label the base before converting.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Profit = 20% of S.P. Let S.P. = ₹100. Profit = ₹20, so C.P. = ₹80.\nProfit on C.P. = 20/80 ×100 = 25%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
